# Group b scenes: Glass Coast, Ember Sea, Skyspine, Pale Basin, the Shattered Choir.
# Questlines here: QW7 Old Bellharbor (N11 -> N13 -> N12), QW2 The Ridge Road (N16), QC10 The Host That Stopped Answering (N39).

# ================================================================ N11 Tidewrack (R03, CH05)
@scene N11_TAM
if flag:n11_tam_told short
set n11_tam_told
say tam | New hands? Good. This coast wrecks a ship a month and gives it back a plank at a time.
say tam | See where a hull glints, where the weed's been scraped off? That's a salvage node. Hand in, and pull.
say tam | Some give iron. Some give tonic tins. Some give a ring off a man nobody's missed. The tide fills them again.
say tam | Try the split in the barge behind me, then the ironwork on the far wreck. Mind your fingers on the ribs.
journal | Tidewrack: salvage nodes glint on wrecks and ruins. Search them; the tide refills them.
end
label short
if phase:post post
say tam | Glints on the hulls. Hand in, pull, and don't ask whose it was.
end
label post
say tam | Since the fault the sea gives back houses. Whole rooms, with the table laid. I pull what I can. I leave the tables.
@end

@scene N11_NODE_A
if flag:n11_node_a empty
set n11_node_a
sfx FX007
say narr | Wedged in the barge's ribs: a sealed tonic tin, and a fist of ore gone green with the sea.
give I002 1
give M001 1
end
label empty
say narr | The split in the hull is picked clean. The tide will bring more.
@end

@scene N11_NODE_B
if flag:n11_node_b empty
set n11_node_b
sfx FX007
say narr | Under the weed, a strongbox hinge and a chain of good iron. The box itself is long gone.
give M001 2
give I009 2
end
label empty
say narr | Scraped ironwork. Nothing left but barnacles.
@end

@scene N11_NETMENDER
if phase:post post
say elder | Nets are for fish. Since the fault I've been mending them for people. We drag the shallows after every high tide.
end
label post
say elder | The Ministry takes a third of all salvage. The tithe barge came to collect it and ran aground. Now it's our roof.
say elder | They still send a man to count. He counts the barge too.
@end

@scene N11_DIVER
if phase:post post
say sailor | I dived the Brine Stair once, south of here. Smugglers' caves. The smugglers are gone and the caves are fuller.
say sailor | Whatever lives in the brood pool took them. There's a pool at the bottom that hums. Don't listen to it.
end
label post
say sailor | The Brine Stair didn't flood with the fault. First place on this coast that didn't get worse. Tells you something.
@end

@scene N11_TITHE
say guard | Salvage tithe, one third to the Crown. Everything the sea returns belongs to the Ministry of Waterways first.
say guard | Yes, including that. Yes, including the barge. I don't write the rules. I just count them.
@end

@scene N11_KID
if phase:post post
say child | I found a boot with a foot in it once. Mum made me put it back.
end
label post
say child | We don't go to the lower beach. Mum says the houses that wash up belong to people who might still want them.
@end

@scene N11_WIDOW
if phase:post post
say survivor | Twenty-two hulls on this beach. My Ander's boat isn't one of them. So he's still out there. That's how I count.
end
label post
say survivor | Forty hulls now. Still not his. I keep counting. Somebody on this coast has to be stubborn.
@end

@scene N11_READER
if phase:post post
say scholar | Every ship has a name on her stern. I copy them into this book. Somebody should know where they ended up.
say scholar | That one's Crown Tithe Barge 11. It ended up as a roof. Some names do better than others.
end
label post
say scholar | I've started a second book, for the houses. Old Bellharbor's lower quay went under whole. Door numbers, mostly.
@end

@scene N11_REFUGEE
say survivor | Bellharbor wanted our papers stamped before we could stay. We walked here instead.
say survivor | The salvagers don't ask for papers. They ask if you can swim, and whether you'll share.
@end

@scene N11_LAMPMAN
say worker | Storm lamps from Lanternfall, up the cliffs north. The lamp makers there light the whole coast.
say worker | Go at dusk. Their night market's the only place I know where the Crown's lamp tax gets paid in jokes.
@end

@scene N11_RUMOR
say sailor | The guild boats run from Belltower Point out to Saltwhistle Isle. Fishing tournament season, if you've the arms.
say sailor | And if a boat ever offers to take you to Gullrock, say no. Nobody lands at Gullrock. Everybody leaves from it.
@end

@scene N11_P_BAILER
say worker | The fault dropped this coast a man's height. We bail the hall every morning and sleep in shifts.
say worker | Still dry, most days. Tidewrack's always been built out of other people's bad luck. We're good at it.
@end

@scene N11_P_LAMPS
say apprentice | I'm from Lanternfall. Was. The cliff went into the sea with the kilns still lit. We watched it burn on the water.
say apprentice | Oda's people went south to Hearthward. I stayed. Somebody here has to know how to make a lamp.
@end

@scene N11_P_SAVED1
say survivor | Five months in the customs vault. We drank the rain through a crack and counted the tides by the bell.
say survivor | When the knocking answered us, I thought I'd finally gone mad. Then a window came down through the dark.
@end

@scene N11_P_SAVED2
say child | The bell had a window. I could see fish looking in. They looked more scared than me.
@end

@scene N11_INN
if phase:post post
say marl | Forty crowns for a bunk in a ship's belly. You'll sleep like the drowned. I mean that kindly.
inn 40
end
label post
say marl | Twenty. Half the town sleeps here now the lower houses flood. Step over the bucket line.
inn 20
@end

@scene N11_DRINKER
say sailor | This inn was the Mary Coyle, out of Veyr. Wenna's grandmother's. She sailed it onto the beach on purpose.
say sailor | Said she was done with the sea and wanted a house that knew how to float.
@end

@scene N11_P_COTS
say patient | The inn gave us the cots by the hearth. Salt water in the lungs. It comes up slow.
@end

@scene N11_TRADER
if phase:post post
say pruett | The sea sells, I buy, you buy. Everybody makes a little, except the sea, which doesn't care.
shop SHOP_N11
end
label post
say pruett | Prices are up. Everything the sea gives back now used to belong to someone who'd like it back.
shop SHOP_N11
@end

@scene N11_CRAFTER
say hesk | Hull iron, sea-glass, brine pearls if you're brave enough to go down the Stair for them.
say hesk | Bring me what the wrecks give up and I'll rivet it into something you can wear. Salvage is just crafting with patience.
@end

@scene N11_CREW
say worker | Wenna runs the crews. Nobody goes into a hull on a rising tide, and nobody goes in alone. Two rules. We keep both.
@end

@scene N11_LEDGER
say clerk | I keep the hall's book: what came up, who brought it, and whose it was, if we can tell.
say clerk | When we can tell, it goes back. The Ministry calls that theft from the Crown. We call it the second rule.
@end

@scene N11_WENNA
if phase:post post
say wenna | Welcome to the Salvagers' Hall. Everything in here came out of the sea, including half my crew.
say wenna | You want work, Tam teaches the nodes. You want trouble, the Brine Stair's south. You want both, stay for supper.
end
label post
if q:QW7 done
if q:QW7:RESOLUTION_READY resolve
if q:QW7:ACTIVE active
if !ch:CH16 notyet
say wenna | You've a ship now. The one that flies. Good. I've a job that needs something that can go where we can't.
say wenna | Old Ossery at Belltower Point hears knocking through his lamp-glass at low tide. Three knocks. A pause. Three.
say wenna | It's coming from under the water, where the Bell Quay used to be. Old Bellharbor. Someone's alive down there.
say dain | After five months?
say wenna | The customs house had a vault. Stone, sealed, above the high-water mark. The quay went down; maybe the vault held.
say wenna | Go to Ossery. He's got the only thing on this coast that's ever been down there and come back up.
quest QW7 ACTIVE ossery
journal | QW7 Old Bellharbor: someone is knocking under the sea at Old Bellharbor. Speak to Keeper Ossery at Belltower Point.
objective | Speak to Keeper Ossery at Belltower Point.
end
label notyet
say wenna | We drag the shallows after every tide. Most of what we pull up is Old Bellharbor. Most of it is furniture.
say wenna | Some of it isn't. If you ever find a way to go deeper than a rope, come and see me.
end
label active
say wenna | Ossery at Belltower Point, and whatever he needs. Every low tide the knocking's a little slower.
end
label resolve
call QW7_RESOLVE
end
label done
say wenna | Eleven for eleven. I've been doing this thirty years and I've never had a count come out whole before.
@end

# ================================================================ QW7 Old Bellharbor (N11 -> N12 -> N13 -> N12 -> N11)
@scene QW7_OSSERY
if q:QW7 done
if qs:QW7:dive dive
if qs:QW7:bell bell
if qs:QW7:glass glass
say ossery | Wenna sent you. Good. Listen. Put your ear to the glass. There.
say narr | Through the lamp room's cold pane, very faint: three knocks. A pause. Three knocks.
say ossery | Every low tide. Coming up the old sea-stair from the drowned quay. Somebody's knocking on stone.
say ossery | This tower's named for a bell, and the bell's a diving bell. My grandfather built it for the pearl beds.
say ossery | It still hangs in the lamp room. But its window cracked the night of the fault. Without a window it's a coffin.
say ossery | The smugglers in the Brine Stair had a diving helmet of real sea-glass. They used it to reach the brood pool.
say ossery | Bring me that glass and I'll set it in the bell. Then we'll need a strong back on the crane, and a braver one inside.
quest QW7 ACTIVE glass
journal | QW7: find the smugglers' sea-glass diving helmet in the Brine Stair (the contraband hold), and bring it to Keeper Ossery.
objective | Find the smugglers' sea-glass helmet in the Brine Stair's contraband hold.
end
label glass
say ossery | The sea-glass, from the Brine Stair's hold. Low tide's in four hours. It's in four hours every day. Hurry anyway.
end
label bell
say ossery | That's the one. Thick as my thumb, not a bubble in it. Hand it here.
say narr | The keeper works the glass into the bell's brass collar with tallow and patience. He does not hurry, and does not stop.
say ossery | There. She'll take six at a time, standing, if they're friends. The tide gives us two drops before it turns.
say ossery | Go down the sea-stair on the point and wait at the bottom rung. I'll lower her to you from the tower.
quest QW7 ACTIVE dive
objective | Descend the sea-stair on Belltower Point; Ossery will lower the diving bell.
end
label dive
say ossery | The sea-stair, on the point. I'll be on the crane. Knock three times when you're in. I'll know.
end
label done
say ossery | Eleven came up in my grandfather's bell. He'd have been insufferable about it. I intend to be.
@end

@scene QW7_GLASS
if !qs:QW7:glass none
say narr | Under the oilcloth, packed in straw: a diving helmet of thick green sea-glass, bolted into a brass collar.
say narr | The smugglers used it to reach the brood pool and come back. Mostly. The collar has a name scratched in: 'ours'.
quest QW7 ACTIVE bell
objective | Bring the sea-glass helmet to Keeper Ossery at Belltower Point.
end
label none
say narr | Old diving gear, a helmet of sea-glass in a brass collar. The smugglers left in a hurry, or not at all.
@end

@scene QW7_STAIR
if qs:QW7:dive dive
if phase:post post
say narr | Iron rungs down the rock to the old fishing ledge. Gulls have made the bottom rung their business.
end
label post
if q:QW7 done
say narr | The sea-stair runs down into dark water. Somewhere below, the Bell Quay. Nothing is knocking now.
end
label done
say narr | The sea-stair runs into the water. Ossery has painted the top rung white: 'Eleven. All of them.'
end
label dive
call QW7_DIVE
@end

@scene QW7_DIVE once
say narr | At the bottom rung the water is black and very still. Then the bell comes down out of the lamp-light, swinging.
say narr | Green glass, brass ribs, a hatch in the floor. The party climbs in. Someone knocks three times on the brass.
fade out 0.6
sfx FX031
say narr | Down. The light goes green, then grey, then goes. Only the glass shows anything: a street, a door, a lamp post, fish.
say narr | Old Bellharbor. The Bell Quay, whole and drowned. Customs House, three doors down. A knocking, very near.
fade in 0.6
say narr | The bell settles on the customs steps. Behind a stone door a pocket of air, and eleven people, and a bell-rope.
say survivor | You came. We heard you. We heard you every day and thought it was the tide lying to us.
say dain | Eleven. The bell takes six, and the tide gives us two drops. Everyone comes up.
say survivor | Then who's first? There are children here. There's Harl, who can't stand. There's me, who wants to go first and hates it.
choice | Send the children and Harl first -> first | Ask them to decide among themselves -> ask
label first
set qw7_chose
say dain | Children and Harl in the first drop. Everyone else holds the rope. We'll be back before the tide turns.
say survivor | ...That's fair. It's fair and I hate it. Go.
goto trip
label ask
set qw7_asked
say dain | You've kept each other alive for five months. You know better than we do. Decide, and we'll carry it.
say narr | They talk for a long minute. The children go first, and Harl, and the woman who wanted to go first stays and holds the rope.
say survivor | We chose. Thank you for letting us. Nobody's let us choose anything since the water came.
label trip
say narr | The first drop rises. Halfway up, the glass goes dark: something long has wrapped itself round the bell.
battle POWX_SEA_6 noflee
say narr | It lets go. The bell breaks the surface in the lamplight and Ossery is already hauling, shouting for blankets.
say narr | The second drop, before the tide turns. Five more, and a bell-rope, and the last of the air in Old Bellharbor.
say ossery | Eleven. Eleven. I'll paint it on the rung.
quest QW7 RESOLUTION_READY rescued
objective | Tell Wenna Coyle in Tidewrack that everyone came up.
@end

@scene QW7_RESOLVE once
say wenna | Eleven? All of them? ...Sit down. No. Stand up. I need to shout at somebody and you're the nearest.
say wenna | Ossery sent me the bell's plans by gull. A pressure skin, ribs, a glass keel. Built big, it'd fit under a ship.
say wenna | Your ship. We fit the Lanternwake with a diving hull, and she goes under the sea like the bell did. Deeper.
say wenna | There are more quays down there. More vaults. Maybe nobody in them. Maybe somebody. Now we can look.
set lanternwake_diving
say narr | (The Lanternwake has a Diving Hull: she can dive and sail under the sea.)
give A118 1
gold 2000
quest QW7 COMPLETED
journal | QW7 complete: everyone came up from Old Bellharbor. The Lanternwake can dive.
@end

# ================================================================ N12 Belltower Point (R03, CH05)
@scene N12_FERRY_NORTH
if phase:post post
if !ch:CH06 tied
say coll | North to Skyspine, first bell, every day the sea lets me. The trigger's at the ferry bell on the Bellharbor road.
say coll | Two hours to the cliffs if the wind's kind. Four if it's Ministry weather. Pay me, not the Ministry.
end
label tied
say coll | Tied up by Ministry order until the archive business is settled. Whatever that means. It means I drink.
end
label post
say coll | Skyspine's still there. The cliffs came down to meet the sea halfway. I run the Hearthward crossing now.
say coll | The timetable's the tide's, not mine. Nobody argues with it. That's the only thing the fault improved.
@end

@scene N12_FERRY_SALT
if phase:post post
say brisa | Saltwhistle boats go when the guild flag flies. Green flag, guild's taking passengers. Red flag, tournament.
say brisa | Black flag, somebody's been lost off Gullrock and we're all out looking. We don't talk about the black flag.
end
label post
say brisa | Saltwhistle kept its flagpole through the fault. The guild flies green every day now, whatever the weather.
say brisa | Doesn't mean passengers. Means 'we're still here'.
@end

@scene N12_TIMEKEEPER
say clerk | Ferry timetable, by tide: north boat at the first bell. Guild boats when the flag flies. Ministry boats never.
say clerk | I also keep the drowning book. Two columns. The second one's always shorter. That's the lighthouse's doing.
@end

@scene N12_GULL
say child | The keeper feeds the gulls fish heads so they'll yell at boats that come in wrong. They're very good at yelling.
@end

@scene N12_MOURNER
if phase:post post
say elder | My husband rang the harbour bell in Bellharbor for thirty years. The Ministry took the bell for inspection.
say elder | He came up here and rang this one instead, in a storm, so the boats could find the point. The storm won.
end
label post
say elder | They've added a line to the cairn for Old Bellharbor. One line for a whole quay. The bronze ran out.
@end

@scene N12_P_RAFT
if q:QW7 done
say survivor | We came off the lower quay on a door when it went. Floated here. The keeper pulled us out with a gaff.
say survivor | He hears things through the lamp-glass. Knocking, he says. Everyone says he's mad. I don't.
end
label done
say survivor | Eleven came up in the keeper's bell. I knew one of them. I'd been lighting a candle for her. I still am. Different reason.
@end

@scene N12_OSSERY
if phase:post post
say ossery | Lighthouse keeper. The Ministry stopped paying for the oil in spring. I light it anyway. Boats don't read budgets.
say ossery | The bell up top's a diving bell. Grandfather built it for the pearl beds. Nobody's dived for pearls in forty years.
end
label post
if q:QW7:ACTIVE qw
if q:QW7 qw
say ossery | The lamp's still lit. The sea's higher, the rocks are new, and there's a whole town under the point now.
say ossery | Some nights I put my ear to the glass and I'd swear I hear knocking. Wenna at Tidewrack says I'm not mad.
end
label qw
call QW7_OSSERY
@end

@scene N12_LAMPROOM
if phase:post post
say narr | The lamp room: a great oil lamp in brass, and above it, slung from a crane arm, a bell tall enough to stand in.
end
label post
say narr | The lamp room. The diving bell hangs from its crane, green glass eye facing the sea. The oil smells like weather.
@end

# ================================================================ N13 The Brine Stair (R03, CH06, boss BX03)
@scene N13_ENTER once
say narr | Sea caves under the coast cliffs. Chalk arrows on the rock, crates rotting on the ledges. The smugglers' road.
say narr | The water here doesn't move with the tide. It moves toward the back of the cave, and stays.
@end

@scene N13_SLUICE_A
if flag:n13_sluice_a done
sfx FX024
shake 0.3
set n13_sluice_a
say narr | The wheel groans round. Somewhere to the west the sea falls away from a gap in the rock with a long sigh.
end
label done
say narr | The west sluice is open. The wheel won't turn back without a crew of six.
@end

@scene N13_SLUICE_B
if flag:n13_sluice_b done
sfx FX024
shake 0.4
set n13_sluice_b
say narr | The second wheel turns. The channel north drains to the ankle, then to wet rock. The way up the Stair is open.
end
label done
say narr | The north channel stays drained.
@end

@scene N13_KEY
if flag:n13_key done
set n13_key
say narr | You take the key from the drowned man's neck. The cord has been tied and retied many times. It was his job.
end
label done
say narr | The drowned smuggler. His hands are folded now. Someone had to.
@end

@scene N13_ROPE
if flag:n13_rope done
sfx FX010
set n13_rope
say narr | You loose the rope ladder. It unrolls into the dark and slaps rock far below: the sea mouth. A short way back.
end
label done
say narr | The rope ladder hangs down to the sea mouth.
@end

@scene N13_LEDGER
say narr | DATE. CARGO. PAID TO HER. Salt, silk, forged passage papers. Paid: one man. One man. One man. Two men (storm).
say narr | The last entry is in a different hand: 'She wants all of us now. We are not paying. God help whoever finds this.'
@end

@scene N13_GHOST
if flag:n13_boss after
say survivor | Forty-one trips. Forty-one men. We told ourselves they were volunteers. We told ourselves a lot of things.
say survivor | She's past the hold, in the brood pool. She sings. Don't sleep. Whatever you do, don't sleep.
end
label after
say survivor | She's quiet. The ledger's closed. Burn it, or read it out loud somewhere. Either's fair.
@end

@scene N13_BOSS once
music M025
say narr | The brood pool is still. Then the water draws back from the edges all at once, toward the centre.
say narr | Something rises with it: a woman's shape to the waist, and below the waist, coils. She is humming a lullaby.
say matriarch | More of them. The men who came before paid in men. What will you pay?
say dain | Nothing. We're closing the account.
say matriarch | Then sleep, little debtors, and I'll write you down.
battle BX03 noflee
set n13_boss
say narr | The coils go slack into the pool. The humming stops in the middle of a bar.
give G112 1
gold 900
rumor | The Brine Stair is quiet. Tidewrack's divers will want to know.
@end

@scene N13_RECEIPTS
say narr | Receipts, hundreds of them, in her hand: a crude drawing of a man on each. Some have names. You take the ones with names.
say narr | One tarred box is marked differently: 'HER SHARE. GULLROCK - UNDER THE STONES.'
say narr | Tidewrack's hall keeps a book for names like these. They will know what to do with them.
@end

# ================================================================ N43 Lanternfall (R03, CH06, pre-fault only)
@scene N43_STALL_GLASS
say ysa | Blown glass, lamp chimneys, lenses for the lighthouse. Hold one to your eye: the whole market's on fire, safely.
shop SHOP_N43
@end

@scene N43_STALL_OIL
say baker | Lamp oil, whale-free, fish-free, mostly-free of Ministry tax. Seal oil for the storm lamps. Don't drink either.
@end

@scene N43_STALL_MOTH
say apprentice | Moth-lamps! A jar, a wick, and a moth that's decided to stay. They only light for people they like.
say apprentice | ...It went dark. Don't take it personally. It went dark for the tax man too.
@end

@scene N43_STALL_CHARM
say elder | Grief lamps. You write a name on the paper shade and hang it in your window. The light says: still counting you.
say elder | Most of mine go to Belltower Point and Tidewrack. The sea's very busy on this coast.
@end

@scene N43_STALL_FOOD
say baker | Lantern buns: baked in the kiln flue while the glass cools. Everything in Lanternfall tastes a bit of smoke. You get used to it.
@end

@scene N43_STALL_MAPS
say scholar | Maps of the coast by lamplight, drawn by people who actually walk it. South of Tidewrack: the Brine Stair.
say scholar | I've marked it 'no'. It's the only honest mark on the map.
@end

@scene N43_LIGHTER
say worker | Lamplighter. I light every lamp in the village at dusk and every one on the cliff stair. Two hundred and six.
say worker | The Ministry charges a tax per flame. So the lamp makers made one flame, and split it two hundred and six ways.
@end

@scene N43_WIDOW
say survivor | The Tallows up the lane lost their boy off the cliff stair in the wind. The whole village lit for him that night.
say survivor | You could see it from the sea, they said. The boats came in by it. That's what a lamp's for.
@end

@scene N43_CHILD
say child | I'm going to be a lamp maker. Oda says my hands are good but my patience is a moth.
@end

@scene N43_GUARD
say guard | Curfew's at the second bell, but nobody's enforced it here in years. You try telling a lamp maker to go to bed.
say guard | The cliff's creaking more this season. The old men say there's a line of builder stone under it. They say it hums.
@end

@scene N43_PILGRIM
say volunteer | I'm carrying grief lamps to Belltower Point for the cairn. Twelve names. I only knew two of them.
@end

@scene N43_CRIER
say clerk | Hear ye: the night market opens when the lamps do! Glass, oil, charms, buns, and whatever Ysa's calling art.
say clerk | By order of the Lampmakers' Moot, the lamp tax will be paid in full, in candle stubs, on the Ministry's steps.
@end

@scene N43_MISER
say noble | I came to buy the Galloway works outright. Oda said she'd sell the moment the sea stopped needing light.
say noble | I'm told that was a no.
@end

@scene N43_CRAFTER
say oda | Galloway Lampworks. Four generations, one kiln, and a great deal of burned hair.
say oda | Bring me sea-glass, good tallow, a bit of ore for the brass, and I'll make you something that burns right.
say oda | The kiln sits on old builder stone. Keeps the heat like nothing else. Hums, some nights. We don't ask it why.
@end

@scene N43_GLASSBOY
say apprentice | Don't touch the gather, it's hotter than it looks. Everything here is hotter than it looks. Especially Oda.
@end

@scene N43_INN
say brannock | The Wick and Wind. Thirty-five crowns, a bed, and a lamp in the window that I'll put out when you ask. Nobody asks.
inn 35
@end

@scene N43_TRAVELLER
say sailor | I came up the cliff stair for the night market and missed my boat three nights running. I think I live here now.
@end

@scene N43_LAMPSELLER
say wick | Tallow and Wick. Lamps, oils, charms. Every lamp we sell is lit once before it leaves, so it knows what it's for.
shop SHOP_N43
@end

@scene N43_BUYER
say noble | I've bought a lamp for every window in my house in Veyr. My neighbours will hate it. That's the point.
@end

# ================================================================ N14 Saltwhistle Isle (R06, CH06): fishers' guild, tournament
# Tournament hooks for the fishing minigame (built by the lead): Tourney Master Oyle is npc id fishing_master.
# N14_TOURNEY sets flag fishing_tournament_open when the party signs up. The minigame sets fishing_rank1 / fishing_rank2
# / fishing_rank3 (or fishing_entered with no placing); the prize scenes N14_PRIZE_GOLD/SILVER/BRONZE are `once`.
@scene N14_TOURNEY
if flag:fishing_rank1,!event:N14_PRIZE_GOLD gold
if flag:fishing_rank2,!event:N14_PRIZE_SILVER silver
if flag:fishing_rank3,!event:N14_PRIZE_BRONZE bronze
if flag:fishing_champion champ
if flag:fishing_tournament_open open
say fishing_master | Tourney Master Oyle. The Saltwhistle Open: one day, one rod, the whole Ember Sea to fish in.
say fishing_master | Biggest catch takes the Silver Hook. Second and third take smaller hooks and the right to complain loudly.
say fishing_master | No entry fee. The guild pays for the tournament out of the fish. The fish have not been consulted.
choice | Sign up -> sign | Not today -> no
label sign
set fishing_tournament_open
say fishing_master | Written in. Your name goes on the board under the ones who've won, and the ones who drowned trying.
say fishing_master | Cast off from any guild pier. When you've a catch worth weighing, bring it to the scales. Good luck. You'll need it.
journal | Saltwhistle Isle: signed up for the fishing tournament. Fish from the guild piers and bring the catch to the scales.
end
label no
say fishing_master | The sea'll be there tomorrow. So will I, unfortunately.
end
label open
say fishing_master | You're entered. Guild piers, one rod, and nobody's allowed to use a net. Yes, Gorse, that means you.
end
label champ
say fishing_master | The champion of Saltwhistle! You're on the board in big letters. Vane's already practising for next year.
end
label gold
call N14_PRIZE_GOLD
end
label silver
call N14_PRIZE_SILVER
end
label bronze
call N14_PRIZE_BRONZE
@end

@scene N14_PRIZE_GOLD once
say fishing_master | Winner of the Saltwhistle Open! Somebody fetch the chalk. Somebody fetch Vane, he'll want to sulk.
say fishing_master | The Silver Hook. It's not silver. It's older than silver on this island. It's been lost overboard nine times.
give A107 1
gold 1500
set fishing_champion
@end

@scene N14_PRIZE_SILVER once
say fishing_master | Second place. A fine fish. Not the finest. You'll hear about the difference for years.
give A108 1
gold 800
@end

@scene N14_PRIZE_BRONZE once
say fishing_master | Third! On the board, in small letters. Everybody starts in small letters.
give I005 2
gold 400
@end

@scene N14_RECORDS
say narr | BIGGEST CATCHES. A grouper, 'the size of Oyle's mother'. A ray 'seen from Belltower Point'. A boot, 'with owner'.
say narr | At the bottom, newer chalk: 'NOT A RECORD: the Crown's fish tax, which is larger every year.'
@end

@scene N14_VANE
if phase:post post
say vane | Guildmaster Vane. Saltwhistle is the guild and the guild is Saltwhistle. Everything else on this rock is fish.
say vane | The Crown's tax on the catch doubled again. So we fish twice as much and eat half as well. That's arithmetic.
say vane | Stay for the tournament. We hold it every year the Crown forgets to ban it.
end
label post
say vane | The Ember Sea boils in the east now. The fish came west, to us, all at once. Too many to land. Too many to sell.
say vane | So we feed Hearthward. Nobody pays. The guild has never been poorer or less ashamed of itself.
@end

@scene N14_RIVAL
say sailor | You're entering? Good. I like to know who I'm beating. I've come second four years running. Fifth year's mine.
@end

@scene N14_SCRIBE
say clerk | I keep the guild rolls: every boat, every hand, every catch. The Crown asks for copies. I send them the old ones.
say clerk | Nobody at the Ministry has noticed they've been reading the same year since I was an apprentice.
@end

@scene N14_P_MOURN
say survivor | We lost six boats the night of the fault. The sea stood up. The guild flies green for them every morning now.
@end

@scene N14_EEL
say baker | Smoked eel, pickled eel, eel pie. We have one fish on this island and we are going to make you like it.
@end

@scene N14_SALTFISH
say keeper | Salt cod from the Pale Basin salt, cured on Saltwhistle racks. Keeps a year. Keeps a family through a bad one.
@end

@scene N14_BAIT
if phase:post post
say apprentice | Bait! Lugworm, sand-flea, and the special, which I'm not allowed to tell you about until you've signed up.
end
label post
say apprentice | Bait's free now. Nobody needs it. The fish jump into the boats. Grandmother says that's a bad sign.
@end

@scene N14_SHELL
say elder | Shell charms. You carry one at sea so the Tide Serpent knows you're a fisher and not a meal. It hasn't been proven wrong.
@end

@scene N14_WEIGHER
say clerk | Weighmaster. I weigh the tournament catches and the Crown's tax catches, on the same scales.
say clerk | The scales are honest. Mostly. The tax weights are a little heavier. We've never found out why. We've never looked.
@end

@scene N14_DRIER
say farmer | Fish on the racks, salt on the fish, wind on the salt. Three days and it's cod you could build a house with.
@end

@scene N14_HOPEFUL
say sailor | I'm going to win the tournament this year. I've been practising on the harbour. The harbour's winning so far.
@end

@scene N14_KID
say child | There's a cove on Gullrock, east, where the smugglers used to go. Mum says it's empty. Mum always says things are empty.
@end

@scene N14_OLDHAND
say elder | Brine Stair smugglers used to run their cargo out of Gullrock. Salt, silk, people. Captain Rask ran the boats.
say elder | She quit one night. Came in on the tide, sank her own cutter in the shallows, and never sailed again. Still lives out there.
@end

@scene N14_TAXMAN
say guard | Fish tax, salt tax, boat tax, pier tax. I'd tax the gulls if I could catch one. The guild says I can't. The guild is correct.
@end

@scene N14_P_REFUGEE
say survivor | I'm from Hearthward, over the water. The guild sends a boat of fish every morning. I came over to say thank you.
say survivor | They put me on the eel stall. I think this is my life now. It's not a bad life.
@end

@scene N14_P_WATCHER
say sailor | I watch the east. Since the fault, the sea over there is red at night. Something moves under it. Something big.
@end

@scene N14_FERRYHAND
say sailor | Guild boats back to the mainland from the south pier, whenever you like. Tell Brisa at Belltower Point we're still here.
@end

@scene N14_INN
if phase:post post
say pearl | The Gutted Herring. Thirty crowns, a hammock, and a bowl of whatever the guild didn't sell.
inn 30
end
label post
say pearl | No charge. Everyone on Saltwhistle's family now, and family pays in washing-up.
inn 0
@end

@scene N14_SINGER
say sailor | ~ Oh the Crown wants a third, and the sea wants the rest, and the fisher gets the bones and the salt in her chest ~
say sailor | It's a cheerful song. You have to hear the second verse.
@end

@scene N14_TACKLE
say gorse | Tackle, stores, salt, oilskins. Everything a fisher needs except luck. Luck's on the shelf behind me; it's not for sale.
shop SHOP_N14
@end

@scene N14_CRAFTER
say ama | Net-wright. I knot nets, and I knot other things: charms, straps, harness. Bring me fish scale, shell and good line.
say ama | Brine pearls from the Stair make the best charms. The divers who bring them back make the best stories.
@end

# ================================================================ N15 Gullrock (R06, secret)
@scene N15_CAIRN
if flag:n13_boss know
say narr | Sea-stones, fitted tight. The gulls scream when you touch them. Whatever is under here, somebody meant it to stay.
end
label know
set n15_cairn
sfx FX024
say narr | GULLROCK - UNDER THE STONES, the Matriarch's receipts said. You pull the stones down one by one. The gulls watch.
say narr | Beneath them, a strongbox, tarred and dry. The smugglers' share. They never came back for it.
@end

@scene N15_NELL
if flag:n13_boss done
say gullrock | Captain Nell Rask. Retired. Don't bother asking from what; the answer's on every Ministry wanted board from here to Veyr.
say gullrock | I ran boats out of the Brine Stair for eleven years. Then I saw what we were paying for the right to use it.
say gullrock | I sank my cutter in the shallows so nobody would sail her back. The others kept paying. Most of them paid in full.
end
label done
say gullrock | You went down the Stair. And she's quiet. ...Sit down. I've been waiting eleven years to pour this for somebody.
say gullrock | The share's under the gull stones, north end. Take it. It was never ours. Maybe it's yours.
@end

@scene N15_LOOKOUT
say child | Nell says if a Ministry boat comes, I whistle twice. If a guild boat comes, I whistle once. I've never whistled.
@end

@scene N15_P_GHOST
say survivor | I rowed for Rask. The fault took the cove's mouth and the sea came in grey and fast.
say survivor | She got the lookout out on her back. I stayed with the boat. I'm still with the boat, it seems.
@end

# ================================================================ N16 Windrest (R04, CH07): shepherds, Brackhorn stables
@scene N16_SHEPHERD
if phase:post post
say farmer | The flocks go up to the high pasture in spring and come down in autumn. The wind decides the days in between.
say farmer | Rocs from Eyrie Hollow take a lamb a week. The queen takes more when she's nesting. We call it rent.
end
label post
say farmer | Half the flock went up the ridge the morning of the fault. The road came down behind them. We hear them some nights.
@end

@scene N16_CARDER
say baker | Carding wool. You comb it till it forgets it was ever on a sheep. Windrest wool goes to High Aerie for cable lining.
say baker | The Crown pays for it by the stone. They weigh it wet. We card it dry. Everybody's cheating a little.
@end

@scene N16_SPINNER
if phase:post post
say elder | I've spun on this step for sixty years. The wind turns the wheel if you let it. I mostly let it.
end
label post
say elder | The wind's changed since the fault. It comes up from the new sea now, wet and salt. The wool won't dry.
say elder | We'll learn. We learned the last wind. It took a hundred years, but we learned it.
@end

@scene N16_KID
say child | I'm going to ride a brackhorn over the mountains. Maddoc says brackhorns don't climb. Maddoc says a lot of things.
@end

@scene N16_ASSESSOR
say guard | Wool assessor, Ministry of Provision. I weigh the clip and take the tithe. They hate me here. It's a small village. I hate me too.
@end

@scene N16_PORTER
say worker | Cable porter. Wool goes down the valley to the High Aerie cable, and the cable takes it over the chasm.
say worker | The Ninefold Shrine keepers run the other cable, up into the clouds. Nobody I know has ridden it and come back talkative.
@end

@scene N16_ROCWATCH
say sailor | I watch the sky for rocs. When I see one I ring this bell and everybody gets their lambs inside.
say sailor | Eyrie Hollow's north-west, up in the crags. Poachers go up for the eggs. Poachers don't come down, mostly.
@end

@scene N16_HORNSMITH
say worker | Brackhorn shoes. Iron for the road, horn for the rock. A shod brackhorn goes anywhere a goat can. Almost.
say worker | The old ridgehorns, the wild ones on the high pasture, go where goats can't. Nobody knows how. They don't tell.
@end

@scene N16_P_FATHER
if q:QW2 done
say survivor | My boy's up on the ridge with the Harl girl and the flock. The road's gone. I shout up every morning.
say survivor | Sometimes somebody shouts back. I tell myself it's him and not the echo.
end
label done
say survivor | He came down on a ram's back, laughing. Laughing! I nearly killed him myself.
@end

@scene N16_P_WIDOW
say survivor | We lost the lower houses when the valley dropped. The cliff kept us. It always has. We're a stubborn cliff.
@end

@scene N16_P_YSOLDE
say ysolde | Old Crook's back in his pen, eating everything. He saved forty sheep and six people and he knows it.
say ysolde | Dad's teaching Bramble the high paths. She hates it. She's brilliant at it.
@end

@scene N16_P_KIN
say farmer | I was on the ridge four months. Snow, sheep, and Ysolde's cooking. I'd take the snow again. I'd take the sheep.
@end

@scene N16_STABLE
if phase:post post
say maddoc | Maddoc Harl. I bred Bramble's mother, and Bramble's grandmother, and I'd know that saddle-sore walk anywhere.
say maddoc | Brackhorns don't climb, they plod. Plod long enough and you're at the top of anything. That's the whole secret.
end
label post
if q:QW2 done
if q:QW2:RESOLUTION_READY resolve
if q:QW2:ACTIVE active
say maddoc | You. You've been places. I need someone who's been places.
say maddoc | My daughter Ysolde took the flock up to the high pasture the morning of the fault. The ridge road came down behind her.
say maddoc | Four months. We see smoke up there some nights. Somebody's alive. The road's broken and I'm too old for rope.
say maddoc | I've untied the shepherds' gate at the top of the village. Go up. Bring her down. Bring whoever's with her.
set qw2_road
quest QW2 ACTIVE ridge
journal | QW2 The Ridge Road: climb the broken ridge road above Windrest and bring down Ysolde Harl and the stranded shepherds.
objective | Climb the ridge road above Windrest to the high pasture.
end
label active
say maddoc | The ridge road, through the gate at the top of the village. There was a winch at the second bend, for hauling hay.
end
label resolve
call QW2_RESOLVE
end
label done
say maddoc | Bramble's shoed with ridgehorn iron now. She'll take you over mountains. Plodding, mind. Always plodding.
@end

@scene N16_STABLEHAND
say apprentice | I muck out, I feed, I get kicked. Brackhorns kick when they like you. I'm very well liked.
@end

@scene N16_INN
if phase:post post
say gwin | The Shorn Fleece. Forty crowns for a bed under two blankets and a sheepskin. Wind's free.
inn 40
end
label post
say gwin | Twenty-five. The blankets are thinner. So is everybody.
inn 25
@end

@scene N16_CABLEMAN
say worker | I rode the Ninefold cable once, up to the Choir anchorage. There's a whole island up there. Crucible, they call it.
say worker | Arena. People pay to fight things. I paid to watch. I don't recommend either.
@end

@scene N16_TRADER
say brannagh | Wool and Iron: coats, spears, horseshoes and knitting needles. Everything a cliff needs.
shop SHOP_N16
@end

@scene N16_CRAFTER
say tolly | Horn, hide and iron. Bring me roc feather, ram's horn, good ore and I'll make you something the wind can't get through.
say tolly | Eyrie Hollow's full of feathers. Full of rocs, too. Those come with the feathers.
@end

# ================================================================ QW2 The Ridge Road (N16, post-fault)
@scene QW2_WINCH
if flag:qw2_beam done
sfx FX024
shake 0.3
set qw2_beam
say narr | The old hay winch groans. A cable runs up the cliff; far above, a long timber beam swings out and drops across the gap.
say narr | It lands with a crack that echoes down the whole valley. It holds.
end
label done
say narr | The hay beam spans the gap in the road. It creaks when the wind leans on it.
@end

@scene QW2_YSOLDE
say ysolde | You came up the road? The road's GONE. ...You came up the road. Dad sent you. Of course he did.
@end

@scene QW2_HURT
say patient | Broke my leg the morning of the fault. Ysolde splinted it with a crook. It's been four months; I'm mostly crook now.
@end

@scene QW2_CAMP once
say narr | The high pasture: tents, a fire pit, a stone pen, and a great many sheep, all of them looking at you.
say ysolde | Ysolde Harl. I've six people, forty sheep, one broken leg and a ram who thinks he's in charge. Is the road open?
say dain | There's a beam across the gap. It holds a person at a time. Not a flock.
say ysolde | Then there's another way. Old Crook knows it. The ridgehorn ram, there. He goes down to the valley every week and comes back.
say ysolde | Goat paths. Ledges, the width of a hand. The rocs hunt there. It'd take all day, with the flock.
say ysolde | Or we carry Davin down the road now, and leave the sheep. They're Dad's whole living. But it's quicker, and Davin's leg is bad.
choice | Carry Davin down the road now -> road | Follow Old Crook with everyone -> crook
label road
set qw2_road_chosen
say dain | Davin first. The flock can wait a day.
say ysolde | ...Right. Right. We'll come back for them. The rocs will have some. Not all.
fade out 0.6
say narr | You carry Davin down in turns across the beam. At dusk the shepherds go back up with rope, and bring down what the rocs left.
fade in 0.6
goto home
label crook
set qw2_crook_chosen
say dain | Everyone and everything. Show us the way, Crook.
say narr | The ram stamps once and steps off the edge onto a ledge you hadn't seen. The flock follows him, one by one.
say narr | Halfway down, with the sheep strung out along the cliff, a shadow crosses the ledge.
battle POWX_R04_4 noflee
say narr | The rocs wheel away. Old Crook does not look back. By dusk, every sheep and every shepherd is in the Windrest pens.
label home
set qw2_down
quest QW2 RESOLUTION_READY home
objective | Tell Maddoc Harl at the Windrest stables that the shepherds are home.
warp N16_R01 from_ridge
@end

@scene QW2_RESOLVE once
say maddoc | Ysolde. ...Ysolde. Don't. Don't say anything. I'll cry on the brackhorns and they'll never respect me again.
if flag:qw2_crook_chosen crook
say maddoc | And the flock? ...We'll rebuild the flock. We can't rebuild her. You chose right.
goto gift
label crook
say maddoc | And the whole flock, down the goat paths behind Old Crook? That ram. That impossible ram.
label gift
say maddoc | Crook's paths. He's been walking them all his life, and now I know where they are. So will Bramble.
say maddoc | Ridgehorn iron on her shoes, and a week on the high paths. She'll carry you over mountains. Plodding.
set brackhorn_ridgehorn
say narr | (Bramble is now a Ridgehorn: she can cross mountains on the world map.)
give A111 1
quest QW2 COMPLETED
journal | QW2 complete: the shepherds are home, and Bramble can cross mountains.
@end

# ================================================================ N17 The Ninefold Shrine (R04, CH07): wind shrine, sky cable
@scene N17_ALTAR
say narr | Nine grooves run out from the basin to nine poles. The wind fills them one after another, never two at once.
say narr | Eight poles have bells. The ninth has only a groove, worn deep, as if something heavy once hung there and rang for a long time.
@end

@scene N17_WINDKEEPER
if phase:post post
say windkeeper | Windkeeper Aun. We keep nine winds here. Eight blow. The ninth stopped before my grandmother's grandmother.
say windkeeper | It came down from the sky, they wrote. Choir-wind. Voices on it, singing orders. Then one day, nothing.
say windkeeper | The sky cable runs up to where it came from: the Choir Anchorage, and the Crucible beyond. We keep the cable too.
end
label post
say windkeeper | Since the fault the eight winds blow all at once. The bells never stop. We sleep in wax.
say windkeeper | And the ninth groove... hums. Just a little. Like a choir that's forgotten the words, humming the tune.
@end

@scene N17_CABLEHAND
say worker | Cable's locked until the Arena crews re-rig the far anchor. The winch key's with the keepers. Talk to Aun.
say worker | Sky cable, basket, two hours up through the clouds. You come out over the Choir Anchorage. Don't look down; there isn't one.
@end

@scene N17_PILGRIM
say volunteer | I walked up from Windrest to ask the West wind for my brother back. He went over the edge in a storm.
say volunteer | The keepers say the West wind comes back as weather. So I'll stand in the rain. It's something.
@end

@scene N17_NOVICE
say apprentice | Novice keeper. I polish bells. Eight bells. There used to be nine. I polish the empty hook too. Aun says it's respect.
@end

@scene N17_P_LISTENER
say elder | I put my ear to the ninth groove every night. Somewhere up there somebody is still conducting.
say elder | The cable's down since the fault. Anyone going up now goes by ship.
@end

@scene N17_ELDERKEEPER
say monk | The ninth wind belonged to the host in the sky: Seraphel. The Choir. They sang the Skyspine's weather once.
say monk | Our book says the Choir stopped answering its own soldiers first. Then it stopped answering us. Then it stopped.
@end

@scene N17_COOK
say baker | Barley, mutton, wind. That's the shrine menu. The wind's the seasoning. You'll taste it by the second bowl.
@end

@scene N17_BOOK
say narr | 'Day of the South wind. Asked for: a safe birth.' 'Day of the North. Asked for: nothing. It has nothing.'
say narr | An old page, in faded ink: 'The Choir-wind has not blown for a year. The keepers still ring its bell. Nobody answers.'
@end

# ================================================================ N18 Eyrie Hollow (R04, CH08, boss BX04)
@scene N18_ENTER once
say narr | The crags above the Skyspine. Rocs' nests crowd every ledge; feathers the length of a man drift in the updraft.
say narr | Somewhere above, very large and very close, something shifts its wings and settles again.
@end

@scene N18_SHUTTER1
if flag:n18_shutter1 done
sfx FX010
set n18_shutter1
say narr | You knock the brace free. The wind-shutter above swings open with a bang and the updraft howls through the chimney.
end
label done
say narr | The brace lies in the snow. The shutter stays open.
@end

@scene N18_SHUTTER2
if flag:n18_shutter2 done
sfx FX010
set n18_shutter2
say narr | The second brace gives. Across the chasm the upper shutter slams open. The poachers built these to keep the rocs out.
end
label done
say narr | The upper shutter hangs open.
@end

@scene N18_FEATHER
if flag:n18_feather done
set n18_feather
say narr | You work the great gold feather free. It smells of the queen: warm, dusty, dangerous.
say narr | Carrying it, you smell like her kin. The nest-walls might let you through.
end
label done
say narr | Branches, and the smell of roc.
@end

@scene N18_POACHER
if flag:n18_boss after
say survivor | Egg-poacher. Was. We came up twelve. Eggs fetch a fortune in Veyr; nobles hatch them for hunting birds.
say survivor | The queen came home early. I hid under the eggs. She sat on me for three days. I'm still up here.
end
label after
say survivor | She's calmer. You didn't take her egg? ...Good. Don't tell anyone in Veyr I said that.
@end

@scene N18_ROPE
if flag:n18_rope done
sfx FX010
set n18_rope
say narr | You let the feather-rope down. It falls away and slaps the rock of Nestfall Ledge far below. A quick way back.
end
label done
say narr | The feather-rope hangs down to Nestfall Ledge.
@end

@scene N18_MOULT
say narr | Drifts of moulted feathers. Beyond the cave mouth, open sky, and a nest built round the whole top of the crag.
say narr | A rest before it would be wise. So would going home. Nobody has ever called poachers wise.
@end

@scene N18_BOSS once
music M025
say narr | The nest is empty. Then the sun goes out: a shadow, vast, sliding across the rim of the nest toward you.
say narr | Aurelle, the Roc Queen, drops onto her nest with a sound like a sail filling, and screams.
say dain | We're not here for the egg!
say narr | She does not care. Her shadow crosses the back of the party, and she rises to stoop.
battle BX04 noflee
set n18_boss
say narr | Aurelle breaks off, bleeding gold, and settles over her egg with her wings spread. She does not strike again.
say narr | She watches you go. That is as close to a truce as a queen will come.
give W131 1
gold 1200
@end

@scene N18_NEST
say narr | One egg, warm as a hearth, and a roc chick the size of a dog, hissing at you with its whole body.
say narr | Aurelle's eye is on you from the rim. You leave the nest alone.
@end

# ================================================================ N19 Lilac Seep (R05, CH09): mineral farmers, herbalist
@scene N19_PICKER
say farmer | You set a stick in the seep, and the brine climbs it and leaves lilac crystal. A month, and it's a flower you can't pick.
say farmer | Nacre's glassmakers buy the crystal. The Crown buys the brine for the relay works. We sell the flowers to anyone who's sad.
@end

@scene N19_RAKER
if phase:post post
say worker | Salt pans. We rake them at dawn before the wind gets up. The wind here takes the skin off your face if you let it.
end
label post
say worker | The basin rang like a bell the night of the fault. The pans cracked. The brine comes up hot now, and it smells.
@end

@scene N19_WARDEN
say elder | Water-warden. The seeps are the co-op's, not mine and not the Crown's. I say which pool rests and which works.
say elder | A pool worked too hard goes bitter. Like a person. We let them rest before that. Mostly we do.
@end

@scene N19_KID
say child | I named my crystal stick Sorrel, after the herbalist. It grew crooked. She says that makes it hers.
@end

@scene N19_ASSESSOR
say guard | Mineral assessor. Relay works want twice the brine this year. The co-op says the seeps can't give it.
say guard | I've been told to write down that they can. I've been writing slowly.
@end

@scene N19_SCHOLAR
say scholar | I've come from Nacre to study the Glass Orchard, west of here. Crystal trees older than the kingdom. They're not trees.
say scholar | What are they? That's the grant application. I'll know when they pay me.
@end

@scene N19_MOURNER
if flag:n21_boss after
say survivor | I'm walking to Sorrowmere, east. You leave a thing at the lake and it takes the memory. Just the one.
say survivor | My daughter. I don't want to forget her. I want to forget the morning she drowned. The lake doesn't take mornings.
end
label after
say survivor | They say the lake's gone quiet. That it gives things back now. I went. I remembered her laughing. I'd forgotten that.
@end

@scene N19_CARTER
say worker | Crystal to Nacre, salt to Cinderwake, brine to the relay works. The cart goes out full and comes back full of paperwork.
@end

@scene N19_STICK
say farmer | This fence keeps the goats off the resting pool. Goats lick the crystal. Then they lie down for a day and see things.
@end

@scene N19_P_DIGGER
say worker | The quake split the east seep. We're digging it out by hand. The crystal came up black on that side. We don't sell it.
@end

@scene N19_P_FORGET
say patient | I went to Sorrowmere after the fault. I wanted to forget the sound. The lake took it. Now I don't know what I'm afraid of.
say patient | I'm still afraid. I just don't know of what. Don't go to the lake to forget. Go to remember.
@end

@scene N19_GRANDMA
say elder | My mother's stick is in that pool. Her mother's too. Every family here has a stick in the seep, growing.
say elder | When I die they'll set mine. It'll grow crooked. I'll insist.
@end

@scene N19_INN
if phase:post post
say hanne | The Resting Pool. Forty-five crowns, a clean bed, and a cup of seep-tea that will make you sleep like a salt pan.
inn 45
end
label post
say hanne | Twenty-five. The tea's still good. The beds are fewer; we gave some to the east seep families.
inn 25
@end

@scene N19_CARAVAN
say sailor | Salt caravan to Nacre. I'd take the long road round the basin if I were you. Sorrowmere's on the short one.
@end

@scene N19_HERBALIST
say herbalist | Sorrel. Herbalist. Seep-mint for the lungs, salt-root for the stomach, lilac-moss for when you can't stop remembering.
say herbalist | I grow everything in pots on the brine. It's bitter. Medicine should be a little bitter; it's honest.
shop SHOP_N19
@end

@scene N19_PATIENT
say patient | Lungs. From the relay works, where they boil the brine. Sorrel's mint helps. The works don't.
@end

@scene N19_CRAFTER
say aldous | I cut crystal and I set it. Brine-glass, lilac crystal, glass-orchard shards if you can find a fallen branch.
say aldous | Bring me those and a bit of ore and I'll set you a charm that hums when danger's close. Or when it's windy. Hard to tell.
@end

@scene N19_CHAIR
if phase:post post
say temperance | Temperance, chair of the co-op. The Crown wants twice the brine. The seeps want rest. I'm in the middle, like chairs are.
say temperance | We've voted to give the Crown exactly what the pools can spare. The Crown hasn't voted. It doesn't need to.
end
label post
say temperance | The relay works are gone. Nobody wants brine now but us. We voted to rest every pool for a season. First time in a hundred years.
@end

@scene N19_CLERK
say clerk | The co-op ledger. Every stick, every family, every pool. When a pool gives, the whole village gives. When it rests, so do we.
@end

# ================================================================ N20 The Glass Orchard (R05, CH09): crystal trees, Vestige lore
@scene N20_ORCHARDIST
if phase:post post
say orchardist | Pellam. Last orchardist. There were forty of us once. The glass trees don't need tending. We tended them anyway.
say orchardist | They're not trees. They grew here before there was a here. The builders planted them, the old songs say, and waited for fruit.
say orchardist | The fruit's empty. Perfect, and empty. My grandmother said they're waiting for something to be poured in.
end
label post
say orchardist | The fault shook half the orchard down. The fallen trees are regrowing already. Out of their own glass. I've never seen it happen that fast.
say orchardist | Something in the Heart Tree woke. It hasn't opened its eyes. It's thinking about it.
@end

@scene N20_CUTTER
say worker | Glass-cutter. Nacre pays for orchard glass by the pound. I only take fallen branches. Pellam watches me like a hawk.
say worker | Fair enough. I watched a man cut a living tree once. It rang for a week. He didn't sleep for a month.
@end

@scene N20_ARCHIVIST
say scholar | The builder marks on the wall: nine circles, one filled. The same ring is on the Ninefold Shrine in the Skyspine.
say scholar | Nine of something. One of them used. I think the orchard grew the Vestiges. I think the Heart Tree is a tenth, not yet picked.
@end

@scene N20_P_PILGRIM
say volunteer | People come here now to sit by the Heart Tree. It hums at night. It's the only thing in the basin that isn't afraid.
@end

@scene N20_HEART
if flag:n20_heart seen
set n20_heart
say narr | You put a hand on the glass. It is warm. Deep inside, the curled shape turns over in its sleep, like a child.
if party:C06 oriel
say narr | For a moment the whole orchard rings, very softly, one note, and then is still.
goto fin
label oriel
say oriel | ...It's dreaming. My blood knows the shape. It dreams in dragon. It's saying: not yet. Ask me again when you've asked yourselves.
label fin
journal | The Glass Orchard: something sleeps in the Heart Tree. The builders called it a seed Vestige. It will not wake without consent.
end
label seen
say narr | The glass is warm. The shape inside sleeps on, curled tight, dreaming of something that has not been decided yet.
@end

# ================================================================ N21 Sorrowmere (R05, CH09, boss BX05)
@scene N21_ENTER once
say narr | A lake so still it holds the sky upside down. Mourners' cairns along the shore. Nobody speaks above a whisper.
say narr | Look into the water too long and you forget, for a heartbeat, why you came. Then you remember. Mostly.
@end

@scene N21_MOURNER1
if flag:n21_boss after
say survivor | I leave something every month. A ribbon, a button. The lake takes a little of the grief each time.
say survivor | It takes a little of him each time too. That's the price. I pay it. I don't know what else to do.
end
label after
say survivor | The lake gave something back. A morning, with him in it. I sat here and cried for an hour and it was the best hour I've had in a year.
@end

@scene N21_MOURNER2
say elder | The lake remembers everything it drowned. Villages, whole ones. It's full of other people's memories and it's heavy with them.
say elder | Whatever lives in the deep mere eats what we leave. Eats, and grows. Don't go out there to forget. It's hungry for forgetting.
@end

@scene N21_FERRYMAN
if flag:n21_raft done
say sailor | No boat. The last one went out to the island of the lamp and didn't come back. The raft's still out there, tied up.
say sailor | If you get out there, cut it loose and push it back to me. I'll owe you the fare.
end
label done
say sailor | You brought the raft back. The island's a short row now, for anyone who needs to sit by the lamp a while.
@end

@scene N21_LAMP1
if flag:n21_lamp1 done
sfx FX010
set n21_lamp1
say narr | You light the drowned lamp. Its light goes down into the water, and the water draws back from it like a held breath.
say narr | The causeway rises out of the lake, wet stones, cut with names.
end
label done
say narr | The lamp burns. The causeway stays above the water.
@end

@scene N21_LAMP2
if flag:n21_lamp2 done
sfx FX010
set n21_lamp2
say narr | The second lamp catches. The lake shrinks back from the far causeway, and the drowned village's roofs break the surface.
end
label done
say narr | The second lamp burns steady.
@end

@scene N21_BELL
if flag:n21_bell done
sfx FX024
set n21_bell
say narr | You pull the rope. Underwater, the chapel bell rings, slow and huge. The whole village shivers.
say narr | The lake remembers the lane out of the village. It gives it back, stone by stone.
end
label done
say narr | The bell rope hangs still. The lane out of the village is dry.
@end

@scene N21_DROWNED
say drowned | Are you here for the bread? Merrow's is closed today. The water came in. It's always today, now.
say drowned | Tell my daughter I kept the shop. Tell her. I can't remember her name. Tell her anyway.
@end

@scene N21_RAFT
if flag:n21_raft done
sfx FX031
set n21_raft
say narr | You cut the raft loose from the island and push it off. It drifts across the black water and grounds on the shore.
say narr | A short way back to the mourners' beach, whenever you need it.
end
label done
say narr | The raft line runs from the island to the shore.
@end

@scene N21_LASTLAMP
say narr | The last lamp burns without oil. In its light the lake is clear: villages on the bottom, streets, a well, a school.
say narr | Beyond the island, the deep mere. The light does not reach that far.
@end

@scene N21_BOSS once
music M025
say narr | The deep mere is flat as glass. Then, at the edge of it, the lake begins to climb the shore.
say narr | Heads rise out of the water: one, then another, long-necked, pale, weeping. Each one is humming a different village's song.
say meremother | Leave it. Leave it with me. You'll feel better. Everyone feels better. Give me the worst thing and walk home light.
say dain | And what happens to the rest of it?
say meremother | I keep it. I keep all of it. I'm so full. Give me yours.
battle BX05 noflee
set n21_boss
say narr | The heads sink one by one. Where each goes down, the water clears, and something rises: a sound, a smell, a morning.
say narr | The lake is giving back what it took. All along the shore, the mourners are standing up.
give A113 1
gold 1500
@end

@scene N21_AFTER
say narr | The deep mere is clear to the bottom. Village after village, small and still. Nobody left in them to forget.
say narr | But the mourners on the shore will remember them now. That will have to be enough.
@end

# ================================================================ N37 Choir Anchorage (SKY, CH07; airship field after the fault)
@scene N37_FIELD
say narr | The old cable yard, painted over with a landing ring. The Lanternwake rides the updraft at her moorings.
choice | Board the Lanternwake -> go | Stay -> stay
label go
warp WORLD_POST helm
label stay
@end

@scene N37_ANCHORMASTER
say anchormaster | Anchormaster Cleave. The cable from the Ninefold Shrine lands here, and the Crucible cable goes on to the Arena isle.
say anchormaster | The Crucible line's shut while the Arena crews re-rig the far anchor. Ask me tomorrow. I've been saying that for a month.
say anchormaster | See the chains on the rock? Stamped SERAPHEL. The host moored here once. It drifted north before my grandfather's day.
@end

@scene N37_CREW
say worker | Cable crew. We grease the drum, check the strands, and try not to look over the edge. The edge is everywhere.
say worker | Some nights you hear singing from the north. No words. Just the tune. The old crew called it the Choir and spat.
@end

@scene N37_TOUT
say noble | Crucible Isle! Fighters from five kingdoms, beasts from the deep sky, and odds I'll give you personally. When the cable runs.
say noble | Until then I'm practising my patter on the cable crew. They've started betting against me.
@end

@scene N37_P_FIELDKEEPER
say pilot | Airfield now. The cable snapped in the fault; half the drum went down with it. We painted a ring where the yard was.
say pilot | Put your ship on the ring and the updraft holds her. North of here, Seraphel's broken up and drifting. Don't moor there.
@end

@scene N37_P_WATCHER
say survivor | I came up the cable the day before the fault, to see the Arena. The cable went, and here I am. Six months.
say survivor | At night the wreck to the north plays one chord. Just one. Over and over. I'd give a lot for a second chord.
@end

@scene N37_P_CLEAVE
say anchormaster | My anchorage is an airfield. My cable's in the clouds. I'm still the anchormaster. Somebody has to hold the chains.
say anchormaster | Seraphel's come close since the fault, drifting on its old chains. If you go in, tell whoever's conducting to stop.
@end

# ================================================================ N39 Seraphel, Fallen Host (SKY, post, boss BX10, QC10)
@scene N39_TO_SHIP
say narr | The host's landing stair drops away beneath you to the Lanternwake's moorings.
choice | Board the Lanternwake -> go | Stay in Seraphel -> stay
label go
warp WORLD_POST helm
label stay
@end

@scene N39_ENTER once
say narr | Seraphel: a sky host of white stone, broken into islands, strung together by chains and old bridges.
say narr | Somewhere inside, an organ holds a single chord. It does not stop for breath. It has not stopped in thirty years.
@end

@scene QC10_HOOK once
say narr | At the broken gate, the Archangel stops. He does not draw his sword. He takes off his helm.
say archangel | This is Seraphel. My host. I commanded its sky road for thirty years, waiting for it to answer me.
say archangel | I thought it stopped answering because it fell. ...No. Look at the statues. It stopped answering long before it fell.
say dain | Do you want to go in?
say archangel | I want to know why. I'll judge it by what I find in there, not by what I was told. Walk with me.
quest QC10 ACTIVE roll
journal | QC10 The Host That Stopped Answering: the Archangel has come home to Seraphel. Learn why the host stopped answering.
objective | Find the host's muster roll in Seraphel.
@end

@scene N39_STOP1
if flag:n39_stop1 done
sfx FX024
set n39_stop1
say narr | You draw the organ stop at the altar. Far off, the chord gains a second note. The north gate's first silver bar hums and lifts.
end
label done
say narr | The stop is drawn.
@end

@scene N39_STOP2
if flag:n39_stop2 done
sfx FX024
set n39_stop2
say narr | The second stop. A third note joins the chord, and for a moment it is almost music. The second silver gate lifts.
end
label done
say narr | The stop is drawn.
@end

@scene N39_ROLL
say narr | Nine hundred names in nine columns. At the head of the first, one name has been struck through with a single clean line.
say narr | Beside it: 'RELIEVED OF COMMAND BY ORDER OF THE CHOIR. NOT TO BE INFORMED.'
if qs:QC10:roll roll
end
label roll
say archangel | That's my name. Relieved. Thirty years ago. They relieved me and didn't tell me, so I would keep holding the road.
say archangel | I held it because nobody told me it had ended. That was the order. That was all I was, to them.
if !party:C08 next
say pip | They made you dead on paper so you'd stay useful. I know that trick. I'm sorry it was done to you.
label next
say archangel | The sentinels on the walk will have been told the same. Let's see what they were told.
quest QC10 ACTIVE posts
objective | Find the sentinels still holding their posts on the Sentinel Walk.
@end

@scene N39_SENTINEL
if qs:QC10:posts decide
if flag:qc10_asked asked
if flag:qc10_ordered ordered
if flag:n39_boss quiet
say hostsoldier | Halt. The host holds this walk until relieved. We have no order to let you pass. We have no order to stop you.
say hostsoldier | We hold.
end
label quiet
say hostsoldier | The chord has changed. Something happened in the loft. We have no order for this. We are holding anyway.
end
label decide
call QC10_DECIDE
end
label asked
say hostsoldier | I am thinking about what the Commander told us. Thirty years is a long time to think about nothing. Now I have something.
end
label ordered
say hostsoldier | Stood down, by the Commander's order. I don't know where to stand now. I will stand here until I do.
@end

@scene QC10_DECIDE once
say hostsoldier | Commander? ...Commander. We held. We were told you had been relieved. We were told to hold until the Choir called.
say hostsoldier | The Choir has not called in thirty years. The conductor still conducts. We hear it every night. Nobody sings.
choice | Order them to stand down -> order | Tell them what you have seen, and let them choose -> tell
label order
set qc10_ordered
say archangel | Sentinels. By my authority as your commander: stand down. You are relieved. All of you.
say hostsoldier | ...By your order, Commander. It is good to have an order again. It has been so long.
say archangel | It was easy for them to take. I gave it for that reason. I hope it was the right one.
goto next
label tell
set qc10_asked
say archangel | I won't order you. Orders are why you are still standing here. I'll tell you what I've seen.
say archangel | Below us the world broke. People are rebuilding it with their hands. Nobody commands them. They choose to.
say archangel | You can hold this walk, or come down, or go anywhere at all. It's yours to judge.
say hostsoldier | ...Nobody has asked us anything in thirty years. May I think about it, Commander?
say archangel | As long as you need. That's the answer I should have been given.
label next
quest QC10 ACTIVE loft
objective | Climb to the choir loft, where the conductor still conducts.
@end

@scene N39_VESTRY
if flag:n39_vestry done
sfx FX009
set n39_vestry
say narr | You lift the latch-bar. The braced arch groans open onto the broken gate: a short way back to the landing stair.
end
label done
say narr | The arch to the broken gate stands open.
@end

@scene N39_BOSS once
music M025
say narr | Rows of empty choir stalls. At the front, a tall winged shape in rotted white, its back to you, conducting.
say narr | The baton moves in perfect time to nothing. Then, far off, the broken organ answers with its one chord.
say seraph | Late. You are all late. Take your places. The Choir will answer. The Choir always answers.
if party:C10 arch
say dain | There's no choir. There's nobody left to sing.
goto fight
label arch
say archangel | Choirmaster. It's me. You relieved me thirty years ago and let me keep holding the road. Why?
say seraph | Commander. You were needed to hold. Holding needs no reasons. Take your place.
label fight
battle BX10 noflee
set n39_boss
say narr | The baton falls. The Seraph's wings fold, and it kneels in the empty loft. Far off, the organ's chord falters.
give I024 1
gold 3000
if q:QC10:ACTIVE,party:C10 qc
end
label qc
call QC10_CLIMAX
@end

@scene QC10_LATE
if q:QC10:ACTIVE,party:C10 qc
say narr | The Seraph kneels by the stand, wings folded, very still. It is waiting for an order nobody here will give.
end
label qc
call QC10_CLIMAX
@end

@scene QC10_CLIMAX once
say seraph | Commander. Give me an order. I have conducted thirty years because no one told me to stop. Tell me to stop.
if flag:qc10_asked asked
say archangel | I ordered the sentinels to stand down. It was kind, and it was easy, and it was one more order.
say archangel | I won't give you one. The Choir stopped answering because it only ever listened to itself. Rest, if you judge it right.
goto after
label asked
say archangel | The sentinels are choosing for themselves. I won't take that from you either. Stop because you see there's no one to sing.
label after
say narr | The Seraph looks out over the empty stalls for a long time. Then it lays the baton down, carefully, and is still.
say archangel | I relieve myself of command. By my own judgment. Nobody else's. ...It's lighter than I thought it would be.
give W004 1
give A020 1
quest QC10 COMPLETED
journal | QC10 complete: the Archangel has relieved himself of Seraphel's last order, by his own judgment.
@end

# ================================================================ N40 The Broken Organ (SKY, secret, post)
@scene N40_KEY
if flag:n40_key done
say narr | One key on the lowest manual is held down by a wedge of white stone. This is the chord. It has sounded for thirty years.
choice | Pull out the wedge -> pull | Leave it -> leave
label pull
set n40_key
sfx FX024
say narr | You work the wedge free. The chord stops. The silence afterward is enormous, and the whole wreck sways on its chains.
say narr | Across the gap, Seraphel's bells shiver once, all together, and go still. Somewhere a sentinel laughs, surprised.
end
label leave
say narr | You leave it. The chord goes on. It will go on after all of you.
end
label done
say narr | The keyboard is silent. The wedge of stone lies on the bench beside it, where you left it.
@end
